---
title: Integral representation of rate function
---

<script>
  import Statement from '$lib/components/statement.svelte';
  import * as needAc from './statements/need-ac.svx';
  import * as fddRfAsIntegral from './statements/fdd-rf-as-integral.svx';
  import * as rfUpperBound from './statements/rf-upper-bound.svx';
  import * as spanAffPartDense from './statements/span-affPart-dense.svx';
  import * as rfLowerBound from './statements/rf-lower-bound.svx';
  import * as ldpIntegral from './statements/ldp-integral.svx';
</script>

<!-- discuss how we intend to get integral form of rate function -->
So far, for each $\stateVar \in \stateSpace^\circ$, [%full](/statements/ldp) provides us a large deviation principle for $(\Prb^\epsilon_\stateVar)_{\epsilon>0}$ with good rate function $\rf_\stateVar$ as in [](tag:eq-rf).
This section is concerned with simplifying the nature of $\rf_\stateVar$ to take a more explicit integral form, comparable to existing principles for other families of stochastic processes.
$$
  \rf_\stateVar(\pathVar) = \left\{\begin{array}{ll}
    \displaystyle \int_0^\infty \ode^*\big(\dot\pathVar(t), \pathVar(t)\big) \rmd t & \pathVar(0) = \stateVar, ~ \pathVar \in \acpathSpace{[0,\infty)}{\stateSpace} \\
    \infty & \text{otherwise}
  \end{array}\right.
$$
To show this, we start by defining a map $\rf$ which composes the initial state through our rate functions $(\rf_\stateVar)_{\stateVar\in\stateSpace}$, to remove the finiteness condition of each $\rf_\stateVar$ in [](tag:eq-rf).
$$
  \rf(\pathVar) \defeq \rf_{\pathVar(0)}(\pathVar) = \sup_{\underline t \vdash \Delta_\pathVar^c} \aff^*\big(\underline t, \pathVar(\underline t), \pathVar(0) \big)
$$
In the following results, we will--without mention--assume evaluations of $\rf(\pathVar)$ for $\pathVar(0) \in \stateSpace^\circ$, so that we can use [%full](/statements/mgf-fdds).
Note that this is at no loss of generality, since we are resolving our rate function $\rf_\stateVar$ for a large deviation principle that already requires $\stateVar \in \stateSpace^\circ$.

<!-- Show that we need \pathVar to be absolutely continuous for it to have finite rate. -->
<Statement {...needAc} />

<!-- Write \affShift as an integral operator. -->
<Statement {...fddRfAsIntegral} />

<!-- "piecewise functions" show rate function sum < rate function integral -->
<Statement {...rfUpperBound} />

<!-- "piecewise functions" are dense in continuous functions -->
<Statement {...spanAffPartDense} />

<!-- rate function integral < rate function sum -->
<Statement {...rfLowerBound} />

<!-- LDP with integral -->
<Statement {...ldpIntegral} />
